## Acknowledgment of Source Materials

This project builds upon work by the preregistration community. All templates and 
skills are derived from or inspired by the following sources (full citations with 
DOIs and links below):

### Key References

**EEG/ERP Preregistration**:
- Govaart, G. H., Schettino, A., Helbling, S., Mehler, D. M. A., Ngiam, W. X. Q., 
  Moreau, D., Chiossi, F., Zanesco, A. P., Yang, Y.-F., Gau, R., Bartlett, J. E., 
  García Alanis, J. C., Gutsell, J. N., Çetinçelik, M., Pavlov, Y. G., Šoškić, A., 
  Ehinger, B. V., Mouseli, P., Algermissen, J., & Paul, E. (2025). EEG ERP 
  Preregistration Template [Preprint]. MetaArXiv. 
  https://doi.org/10.31222/osf.io/4nvpt

- Clayson, P. E., Carbine, K. A., & Larson, M. J. (2021). Making ERP Research More 
  Transparent: Guidelines for Preregistration. International Journal of Psychophysiology, 
  161, 55-67. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijpsycho.2021.02.016

- Šoškić, A., Ković, V., Algermissen, J., et al. (2025). ARTEM-IS for ERP: Agreed 
  Reporting Template for EEG Methodology—International Standard for Event-Related 
  Potential Experiments. Psychophysiology, 62(12), e70187. 
  https://doi.org/10.1111/psyp.70187

**fMRI Preregistration**:
- Beyer, F., Flannery, J., Gau, R., Janssen, L., Schaare, L., Hartmann, H., 
  Nilsonne, G., Martin, S., Khalil, A., Lipp, I., Puhlmann, L., Heinrichs, H., 
  Mohamed, A., Herholz, P., Sicorello, M., & Panagoulas, E. (2021). A fMRI 
  pre-registration template. PsychArchives. 
  https://doi.org/10.23668/PSYCHARCHIVES.5121

**Online Behavioral Preregistration (PRP-QUANT)**:
- Preregistration Task Force. (2020). Preregistration Standards for Psychology - 
  the Psychological Research Preregistration-Quantitative (aka PRP-QUANT) Template. 
  ZPID (Leibniz Institute for Psychology). 
  https://doi.org/10.23668/psycharchives.4584

- Bosnjak, M., Fiebach, C. J., Mellor, D., et al. (2022). A template for 
  preregistration of quantitative research in psychology: Report of the joint 
  psychological societies preregistration task force. American Psychologist, 77(4), 
  602-615. https://doi.org/10.1037/amp0000879
  PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34807636/

**Psychophysiology Preregistration**:
- Boucsein, W., Fowles, D. C., Grimnes, S., et al. (2012). Publication 
  recommendations for electrodermal measurements. Psychophysiology, 49(8), 1017-1034.
  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-8986.2012.01384.x

- Task Force of the European Society of Cardiology and the North American Society 
  of Pacing and Electrophysiology. (1996). Heart rate variability: Standards of 
  measurement, physiological interpretation, and clinical use. European Heart Journal, 
  17(3), 354-381. https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.eurheartj.a014868

- Fridlund, A. J., & Cacioppo, J. T. (1986). Guidelines for human electromyographic 
  research. Psychophysiology, 23(5), 567-589.
  https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-8986.1986.tb00676.x

- Cacioppo, J. T., Tassinary, L. G., & Berntson, G. G. (Eds.). (2007). 
  Handbook of Psychophysiology (3rd ed.). Cambridge University Press.
  ISBN: 978-0-521-84471-0

### Organizations & Communities

- **Center for Open Science (COS)**: https://www.cos.io/
  OSF Registries and preregistration initiative

- **Leibniz Institute for Psychology (ZPID)**: https://www.zpid.de/
  PsychArchives and PreReg resources at https://prereg-psych.org/

- **Society for Psychophysiological Research (SPR)**: https://www.sprweb.org/
  Psychophysiology standards and guidelines

### Platforms for Registration

- **OSF Registries**: https://osf.io/registries
- **AsPredicted**: https://aspredicted.org/
- **PsychArchives**: https://www.psycharchives.org/
- **ClinicalTrials.gov**: https://clinicaltrials.gov/

### Related Standards & Specifications

- **BIDS (Brain Imaging Data Structure)**: https://bids-standard.github.io/
- **jsPsych**: https://www.jspsych.org/
- **Qualtrics**: https://www.qualtrics.com/
- **Pavlovia**: https://pavlovia.org/

All referenced works are cited with proper attribution throughout this project.
The authors and contributors acknowledge the tremendous collaborative effort by 
the open science community to advance transparent, reproducible research.
